WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
A neuron fires faster before a choice, but does it cause it?

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:08.400
A monkey watches moving dots and moves its eyes to answer.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.900
On average, a neuron toward the top and back of its brain fires faster and faster.

4
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:19.640
Yet recording spikes alone cannot prove that region decides.

5
00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:26.983
To test cause, you stop only listening and activate neurons yourself.

6
00:00:28.503 --> 00:00:33.183
Small currents through electrodes, or magnetic pulses, trigger spikes directly.

7
00:00:34.543 --> 00:00:38.623
Every sense ends in the same currency: trains of identical spikes.

8
00:00:40.123 --> 00:00:42.743
Spike rate and the number of active fibres say how much.

9
00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:49.277
A cochlear implant uses that code, threading electrode contacts into the cochlea,

10
00:00:49.677 --> 00:00:51.117
the inner ear's hearing coil.

11
00:00:52.417 --> 00:00:57.417
It skips damaged hair cells, the ear's sensors, and excites the auditory nerve

12
00:00:57.417 --> 00:01:01.917
directly. Speech often sounds harsh or robotic at first.

13
00:01:03.237 --> 00:01:07.697
Even 22 contacts do not carry all the information normal hearing does.

14
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:13.187
In the eye, an epiretinal grid sits on the retina's inner surface.

15
00:01:14.687 --> 00:01:19.347
Asked to draw what one electrode made them see, users often drew elongated or

16
00:01:19.347 --> 00:01:21.267
distorted shapes, not a dot.

17
00:01:22.707 --> 00:01:27.027
Current spread blurs neighbouring groups together; current reaching wrong neurons

18
00:01:27.027 --> 00:01:28.247
misplaces the signal.

19
00:01:29.527 --> 00:01:34.627
Stimulation delivers detail only as far as it drives distinct groups, each in the

20
00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:35.627
right place.

21
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:41.580
Try a toy case: suppose 22 contacts lie along about 16 mm.

22
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:48.580
Give each contact's electric field an illustrative width of 1.75 mm.

23
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.140
Roughly how many separate channels fit before fields overlap?

24
00:01:58.783 --> 00:02:02.363
Length divided by field width gives a rough channel count.

25
00:02:03.763 --> 00:02:10.263
16 mm ÷ 1.75 mm ≈ 9, far fewer than 22.

26
00:02:11.703 --> 00:02:16.443
Overlapping fields excite overlapping neuron groups, called channel interaction.

27
00:02:17.743 --> 00:02:21.883
This is a toy picture, not a prediction: real fields fade gradually.

28
00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:27.837
So counting contacts misleads; you must measure what the user can tell apart.

29
00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:31.737
No single channel count fits every listener.

30
00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:38.137
Neural survival, the distance from contacts to neurons, and the array's reach all

31
00:02:38.137 --> 00:02:43.337
matter. Many users also understand much more after weeks to months.

32
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:48.057
Switching channels on and off tested this directly in listeners.

33
00:02:49.537 --> 00:02:53.917
In 2020, Berg and colleagues tested 18 adult implant users.

34
00:02:55.357 --> 00:02:59.917
Speech recognition reached a plateau, no longer rising, at 8 to 10 channels.

35
00:03:01.017 --> 00:03:04.657
Yet one plateau does not mean extra contacts do nothing.

36
00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:12.417
In 2017, Schvartz-Leyzac, Zwolan and Pfingst cut active electrodes from 20 to 8 in

37
00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:18.497
seven adults. Their speech-in-noise threshold worsened: speech had to be louder

38
00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:21.057
relative to background noise to be understood.

39
00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:26.367
Helping in noise does not mean every contact acts independently.

40
00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:33.507
Whether probing a decision or restoring a sense, stimulation is only as precise as

41
00:03:33.507 --> 00:03:35.187
the distinct neurons it drives.

42
00:03:36.807 --> 00:03:38.867
Behaviour then shows what those neurons do.
